---
title: Greek Yogurt Tuna Casserole with Cheddar (No Canned Soup)
source: claude
servings: 4
tags: [quick]
---

> A lighter tuna casserole made from scratch: a quick roux-based sauce with Greek yogurt and sharp cheddar, no canned soup.

Heat the oven to 375°F (190°C). Grease a #9x13-inch baking dish{}.

Cook @egg noodles{8%oz}(or elbow pasta) in a #pot{} of salted boiling water until just shy of al dente, 1–2 minutes less than the package says, about ~{8%minutes}. They finish cooking in the oven. Drain and set aside.

Melt @butter{3%tbsp} in a #large skillet{} over medium heat. Add @onion{1}(small, diced) and @?mushroom{8%oz}(sliced) and cook until softened and lightly browned, about ~{6%minutes}. Add @garlic{2%cloves}(minced) and cook ~{1%minute} more, until fragrant.

Sprinkle @flour{3%tbsp} over the vegetables and stir constantly for ~{1%minute} to cook out the raw flour taste.

Slowly whisk in @milk{1.5%cups} and @chicken broth{0.8%cups}(or vegetable broth), scraping up any browned bits. Cook, stirring often, until it is thick enough to coat the back of a spoon, about ~{5%minutes}.

Take the pan off the heat and let it cool for a minute or two, then stir in @greek yogurt{1%cup}(plain), @lemon juice{1%tbsp} and @dijon mustard{1%tsp} until smooth. Adding the yogurt off the heat keeps it from splitting.

In a #large bowl{}, mix the noodles and sauce with @tuna{10%oz}(2 cans, 5 oz each, drained), @frozen peas{1%cup}, @cheddar cheese{1.5%cups}(sharp, shredded), @salt{0.5%tsp} and @black pepper{0.3%tsp}. Spread evenly in the baking dish.

Toss @panko breadcrumbs{0.5%cup} with @butter{1%tbsp}(melted) and sprinkle over the casserole.

Bake uncovered until bubbly and the topping is golden brown. Let it rest ~{5%minutes} before serving.

> Mushrooms are optional but give the classic tuna-casserole flavor that canned mushroom soup used to. If you skip them, add an extra splash of broth.
